#!/bin/sh
# Tell the host on the console how much memory the job really needed and whether the kernel
# killed anything for lack of it: the host sees only pages the VM touched, its file cache
# included, and the kernel's own out-of-memory message is below the console's log level.
# Needed is MemTotal less MemAvailable, so cache the kernel can drop does not count.
# Shell builtins only: a fork can fail in the very squeeze this reports (ci_supervisor.py
# reads the line). A line goes out when the peak grows 16 MiB or a kill happens, and every
# 30 s, on a fresh line in case another writer left one half done.
peak=0 said=-16 kills=-1 ticks=0
while :; do
  while read -r key value _; do
    case $key in MemTotal:) total=$((value / 1024)) ;; MemAvailable:) avail=$((value / 1024)) ;; esac
  done < /proc/meminfo
  now=0
  while read -r key value; do [ "$key" = oom_kill ] && now=$value; done < /proc/vmstat
  used=$((total - avail))
  [ "$used" -gt "$peak" ] && peak=$used
  if [ $((peak - said)) -ge 16 ] || [ "$now" != "$kills" ] || [ $((ticks % 15)) -eq 0 ]; then
    printf '\nHOMELAB-MEM 1 %s %s %s\n' "$peak" "$total" "$now"
    said=$peak kills=$now
  fi
  ticks=$((ticks + 1))
  sleep 2
done
